 ],
 "literal": "As the great beauty of my flower-like kohl-applied eyes is lost, / and on top of my soft shoulders adorned with beautiful bangles becoming thin, / it becomes changed indeed, O friend, my endurance indeed, / with the man of the land who is difficult to understand, / the small, good friendship that I made.",
 "translation": "O friend! As the great beauty of my flower-like, kohl-painted eyes fades away, and on top of that, my soft shoulders adorned with beautiful bangles grow thin, my very endurance has failed me. All because of this small, sweet love that I made with the lord of the mountain land, whose mind is so difficult to understand.",
 "modern": "தோழியே! மலர் போன்ற மை தீட்டிய என் கண்களின் சிறந்த அழகு அழியுமாறு, வளையல்கள் அணிந்த மென்மையான என் தோள்கள் மெலிந்து போனதன் மேலே, என் பொறுமையும் அழிந்து போகின்றது. அறிந்துகொள்வதற்கு எளியவன் அல்லாத மலைநாட்டினனோடு நான் செய்து கொண்ட சிறிய, நல்ல நட்பினால் தான் இது நேர்ந்தது.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her close friend in a state of deep distress caused by her separation from the hero. She describes how her physical appearance is deteriorating: the beauty of her flower-like eyes is fading, and her soft shoulders are thinning so much that her bangles are slipping off. On top of these physical changes, she laments that her inner strength and endurance have completely failed. She attributes all this suffering to the 'small, good friendship' she entered into with the lord of the mountain land. She notes that his mind and intentions are difficult to comprehend, reflecting her anxiety over his delay in marrying her. This poem belongs to the Kuriñci tiṇai, where the secret union and the subsequent pain of separation are central themes.",
